1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,392
How do thieves steal card numbers without breaking into any database?

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:10,128
They add one line of script to a payment page, and copy every number as the buyer types it.

3
00:00:10,178 --> 00:00:12,000
That is web skimming.

4
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:17,856
Our card frame is the bank's card reader on the shop's counter, and like a real reader, it has seals.

5
00:00:17,906 --> 00:00:21,096
Only the page its session names may hold the frame.

6
00:00:21,146 --> 00:00:27,672
The frame runs nothing but its own two files: its security policy forbids every other script.

7
00:00:27,722 --> 00:00:31,800
Each file carries its own fingerprint, so a changed file simply does not run.

8
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:37,056
The frame hands back only the token and the masked number, and only to that one page.

9
00:00:37,106 --> 00:00:42,048
When the browser blocks something, it reports it, so a tampering attempt is seen.

10
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:47,616
And the shop's page around the frame is watched too, because a skimmer there could still trick the buyer.

11
00:00:47,666 --> 00:00:50,232
Next: the two doors into the locked room.
